1
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:08,500
Spend Thanksgiving with good ol' Charlie Brown!

2
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
A Charlie Brown Thanksgiving is a animation, comedy,
family film directed by Bill Melendez.

3
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:13,500
Turkey, cranberries, pumpkin pie...

4
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
and the Peanuts gang to share them with.

5
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:18,500
This is going to be the greatest Thanksgiving ever!

6
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
The fun begins when Peppermint Patty invites herself and her pals to
Charlie Brown's house for a REALLY big turkey party.

7
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:23,500
Good grief!

8
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,000
All our hero can cook is cold cereal and maybe toast.

9
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:28,500
Is Charlie Brown doomed?

10
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Not when Linus, Snoopy and Woodstock chip in to save the (Thanksgiving) Day.

11
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:33,500
With such good friends, Charlie Brown - and all of us -
have so many reasons to be thankful.

12
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Charlie Brown (voice), played by Todd Barbee.

13
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:38,500
Lucy van Pelt (voice), played by Robin Kohn.

14
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Linus van Pelt (voice), played by Stephen Shea.

15
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:43,500
Sally Brown (voice), played by Hilary Momberger-Powers.

16
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Peppermint Patty (voice), played by Christopher DeFaria.

17
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:48,500
Marcie (voice), played by Jimmy Ahrens.

18
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:51,000
A Charlie Brown Thanksgiving is a 1973 American television special
based upon the comic strip Peanuts, by Charles M.

19
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:53,500
The tenth prime-time animated television special in the Peanuts, it originally aired on the
CBS network on November 20, 1973, and won an Emmy Award the following year.

20
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:56,000
It was the third holiday special after A Charlie Brown Christmas in
1965 and It's the Great Pumpkin, Charlie Brown in 1966.

21
00:21:52,500 --> 00:21:58,500
Except for the opening football gag, it is the first Peanuts TV special to
have a completely original script without relying on the strip.

22
00:22:55,000 --> 00:23:01,000
The film explores themes of holiday, thanksgiving, boy and dog, jellybean.

23
00:23:57,500 --> 00:24:03,500
The tone is lighthearted.
